{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 16,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.732,
            0.064,
            0.834,
            0.081
          ],
          "content": "Prime Time"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.12,
            0.833,
            0.164
          ],
          "content": "It is easy to find factors of small numbers. How do we find factors of a large number?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.166,
            0.833,
            0.209
          ],
          "content": "Let us take 8560. Does it have any factors from 2 to 10 (2, 3, 4, 5, ..., 9, 10)?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.212,
            0.834,
            0.257
          ],
          "content": "It is easy to check if some of these numbers are factors or not without doing long division. Can you find them?"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.269,
            0.325,
            0.29
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Divisibility by 10"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.292,
            0.76,
            0.336
          ],
          "content": "Let us take 10. Is 8560 divisible by 10? This is another way of asking if 10 is a factor of 8560."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.143,
            0.338,
            0.752,
            0.359
          ],
          "content": "For this, we can look at the pattern in the multiples of 10."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.361,
            0.833,
            0.405
          ],
          "content": "The first few multiples of 10 are: 10, 20, 30, 40, … Continue this sequence and observe the pattern."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.406,
            0.833,
            0.45
          ],
          "content": "Is 125 a multiple of 10? Will this number appear in the previous sequence? Why or why not?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.143,
            0.452,
            0.641,
            0.473
          ],
          "content": "Can you now answer if 8560 is divisible by 10?"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.482,
            0.413,
            0.503
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Consider this statement:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.505,
            0.759,
            0.55
          ],
          "content": "Numbers that are divisible by 10 are those that end with ‘0’. Do you agree?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.777,
            0.486,
            0.862,
            0.547
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_16_image_12.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.561,
            0.311,
            0.583
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Divisibility by 5"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.585,
            0.833,
            0.629
          ],
          "content": "The number 5 is another number whose divisibility can easily be checked. How do we do it?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.632,
            0.834,
            0.676
          ],
          "content": "Explore by listing down the multiples: 5, 10, 15, 20, 25, ... What do you observe about these numbers? Do you see a pattern in the last digit?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.677,
            0.834,
            0.721
          ],
          "content": "What is the largest number less than 399 that is divisible by 5? Is 8560 divisible by 5?"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.729,
            0.413,
            0.751
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Consider this statement:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.752,
            0.762,
            0.797
          ],
          "content": "Numbers that are divisible by 5 are those that end with either a ‘0’ or a ‘5’. Do you agree?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.777,
            0.732,
            0.864,
            0.794
          ],
          "content": "",
